[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Design Patterns in C# - A Practical Guide"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Design patterns are reusable solutions to commonly occurring problems in software design. They represent best practices evolved over time and provide a common vocabulary for developers. This tutorial explores several fundamental design patterns with C# examples."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Singleton Pattern"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"The Singleton pattern ensures that a class has only one instance and provides a global point of access to it. This is useful when exactly one object is needed to coordinate actions across the system."},{"order":5,"type":"code","text":"public sealed class Singleton
{
    private static readonly Singleton instance = new Singleton();

    private Singleton() { }

    public static Singleton Instance
    {
        get { return instance; }
    }

    public void DoSomething()
    {
        Console.WriteLine("Singleton is doing something!");
    }
}"},{"order":6,"type":"paragraph","text":"In this example, the 'Singleton' class has a private constructor, preventing direct instantiation. The 'instance' field is a static readonly field, ensuring that it's initialized only once. The 'Instance' property provides global access to the single instance."},{"order":7,"type":"subheading","text":"Factory Pattern"},{"order":8,"type":"paragraph","text":"The Factory pattern provides an interface for creating objects without specifying their concrete classes. It promotes loose coupling and allows for easy extension and modification of object creation logic."},{"order":9,"type":"code","text":"public interface IProduct
{
    void Operation();
}

public class ConcreteProductA : IProduct
{
    public void Operation()
    {
        Console.WriteLine("ConcreteProductA Operation");
    }
}

public class ConcreteProductB : IProduct
{
    public void Operation()
    {
        Console.WriteLine("ConcreteProductB Operation");
    }
}

public abstract class Factory
{
    public abstract IProduct CreateProduct();
}

public class ConcreteFactoryA : Factory
{
    public override IProduct CreateProduct()
    {
        return new ConcreteProductA();
    }
}

public class ConcreteFactoryB : Factory
{
    public override IProduct CreateProduct()
    {
        return new ConcreteProductB();
    }
}"},{"order":10,"type":"paragraph","text":"Here, 'IProduct' is an interface for products, and 'ConcreteProductA' and 'ConcreteProductB' are concrete implementations. The 'Factory' class defines an abstract 'CreateProduct' method, which is implemented by concrete factories to create specific product instances."},{"order":11,"type":"subheading","text":"Observer Pattern"},{"order":12,"type":"paragraph","text":"The Observer pattern defines a one-to-many dependency between objects so that when one object changes state, all its dependents are notified and updated automatically. It's used to implement distributed event handling systems."},{"order":13,"type":"code","text":"public interface IObserver
{
    void Update(string message);
}

public interface IObservable
{
    void AddObserver(IObserver observer);
    void RemoveObserver(IObserver observer);
    void NotifyObservers(string message);
}

public class ConcreteObservable : IObservable
{
    private List<IObserver> observers = new List<IObserver>();

    public void AddObserver(IObserver observer)
    {
        observers.Add(observer);
    }

    public void RemoveObserver(IObserver observer)
    {
        observers.Remove(observer);
    }

    public void NotifyObservers(string message)
    {
        foreach (var observer in observers)
        {
            observer.Update(message);
        }
    }

    public void DoSomething(string message)
    {
        Console.WriteLine("Observable is doing something and notifying...");
        NotifyObservers(message);
    }
}

public class ConcreteObserver : IObserver
{
    private string name;

    public ConcreteObserver(string name)
    {
        this.name = name;
    }

    public void Update(string message)
    {
        Console.WriteLine($"{name} received message: {message}");
    }
}"},{"order":14,"type":"paragraph","text":"In this example, 'IObservable' defines methods for adding, removing, and notifying observers. 'IObserver' defines the 'Update' method, which is called when the observable's state changes. 'ConcreteObservable' maintains a list of observers and notifies them when its state changes. 'ConcreteObserver' implements the 'IObserver' interface and receives updates."},{"order":15,"type":"subheading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":16,"type":"paragraph","text":"These are just a few examples of the many design patterns available. Understanding and applying design patterns can significantly improve the maintainability, reusability, and scalability of your C# applications. Remember to choose the right pattern for the specific problem you are trying to solve."}]
